ers that showed  over 25.4 million people worldwide are  now on HIV treatment but we really  decided to say well how bad could it get  with Kovac what happens for example if  there were a six-month interruption in  all HIV treatment services in  sub-saharan Africa now we don't plan on  that happening but we have to look at  that sort of bad case scenario and doing  some modeling along with w-h-o and the  HIV modeling consortium we found that a  six-month interruption and treatment  services would lead to an additional an  additional 500,000 deaths from AIDS in  this year alone that would double the  number of deaths in sub-saharan Africa  and actually set us back to 2008  response levels so we lose a decade of  progress in just one year Chenin are  people with HIV more likely to suffer  from a severe kovat 19 infection you  know we're learning as we go on that  there is some new data out of South  Africa which is one of the first  countries affected by kovat that also  has a very large HIV epidemic and they  are seeing that there is some additional  severity and people living with HIV who  get Kovan it's not as bad as for people  who have diabetes or existing lung  disease but of course people living with  HIV are aging and have those things too  so it is particularly important that  people living with HIV take precautions  to try to avoid getting infected to  begin with and that we hope to learn  more as you know treatments evolve  vaccines evolve etc now speaking of  precautions there are a check  patients that are avoiding hospitals out  of fear of contracting the coronavirus  how big of a problem is that well it's a  big problem if we're not offering other  solutions so one thing about HIV care is  for a number of years now whu-oh  treatment guidelines have really  promoted this idea of differentiated  service delivery or community-based  delivery so that someone living with HIV  doesn't have to go to the clinic every  month or every three months just for  routine care the idea that someone  living with HIV has their HIV under  control could get three months of their  medications in one visit and then not  have to go back and forth but a lot of  countries before Kovan we're really slow  and implementing those kind of practices  fortunately we have seen a major scale  up in a number of countries of putting  those practices into place multi-month  dispensing community based delivery  community-based care long distance  telephone consultations and those kind  of engagements for questions so in  countries that are moving to put those  alternatives into place we're seeing a  lot less interruption of people services  but in countries that are not moving to  that community love community based  models it's a problem Shanna nadir  deputy executive director of un AIDS  thank you for speaking to DW and now it  is time for your questions about covent  19 and our science correspondent derrick  williams as usual doing his best to  answer them I've heard researchers talk  about trying to mute the immune response  but also saying that a younger stronger  immune system is more successful in  fighting off cough in 19 isn't that a  contradiction  to understand the apparent contradiction  here you have to understand that there  are are different stages of kovat 19  that that patients can go through  the first is usually an initial  infection in the upper respiratory tract  and and that's usually where it stays  with most people that's where having a  strong fast-acting immune system is  really great it's it's able to keep the  disease in check and eventually wipe it  out in some cases it keeps it so  effectively in check that you might not  even notice that you had it but in more  severe forms of the disease if the virus  eventually makes it to the lungs or to  other organs it can kick in a severe  immune response and and when that  happens the immune system can start  attacking not just disease tissue but  but healthy tissue as well that's when  doctors have to step in and and try to  mute the immune response with drugs  because if it spirals out of control it  can kill you but striking a balance  between letting the immune system do its  job and and reining it in if it spins  out of control is pretty tricky can you  tell us more about dexamethasone  dexamethasone is one of the drugs that  doctors now prescribe at that critical  stage of kovat 19 that I just mentioned  when the immune system has gone into  overdrive and starts attacking healthy  tissue in the body a major study in  Britain that's still in peer review  discovered that in critically ill  patients who need respiratory support  the compound lowers mortality by between  a fifth and and a third which is  actually pretty huge dexamethasone is  what's known as a corticosteroid and  it's widely used to treat many different  conditions that cause painful or  dangerous inflammation it's also an  immunosuppressant so it can help calm  down the immune system when it's running  amok but that also makes doctors wary  about about giving patients the drug too  early  when the immune system needs to be  active it's it's not a new drug but one  that's been approved for use for for  well over fifty years now so so it's  cheap it's widely available and perhaps  most importantly on production of it can  be can be ramped up quickly  [Music]
 

